| editorial =
'''The word on the ballot'''

In Noto, the word is "accountability." Misaki Haruka put it there. Koda Genichi put "stability" there. On July 26, 280,000 voters will choose which word matters.

This is the first by-election since the IAA became law. The coalition's entire agenda — accountability, sovereignty, public interest — is on the ballot in a prefecture that lived through a red tide crisis, a ¥2B supplemental, and a red tide response that failed before it succeeded. Noto knows what government failure looks like. Noto knows what recovery looks like. The question is which one Noto remembers.

